TASK:{"title":"AERO9500 Final Report (Part 2) – Space Mission Design","overview":"Students design a preliminary space mission benefiting Australia, covering the space segment: mission purpose, spacecraft count, users, launch site and vehicle, orbit and orbital elements, manoeuvres, ground control and communications, mission duration and end-of-life. Design may use STK, PIGI or approved software, supplemented by hand calculations, and is reported as a concise technical report with supporting screenshots and software files."}
RUBRIC:[{"id":"C1","name":"Introduction: mission concept and rationale - Include the following required information: • What is the spacecraft's primary mission? • Why is the mission needed? • How many spacecraft are used and why? • Who are the users (operator and customers)?","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Addresses all requirements to a high standard. Detailed descriptions and discussions are included. References to literature as appropriate."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Addresses some requirements. Descriptions may feature lack of specific details and/or incomplete referencing."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Many or all requirements are not addressed. Poor descriptions or lack of appropriate referencing."}]},{"id":"C2","name":"Main body: mission design - Present a summary of the mission design using software and/or hand calculations as appropriate - Include relevant screenshots and software files (in the main body of the report and/or appendix) - Include the following information: • Where it will launch from, and using which launch vehicle? Why did you choose this option? • What type of orbit will you use? What are its characteristics and orbital elements? • Will you need to perform any orbital maneuvers? • Which ground control facilities will you use? • How often will your spacecraft be able to communicate with ground control? • How long will the mission last, and what are your plans for EOL?","weight":60,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Demonstrates high level understanding of the design procedure. Design clearly addresses mission and course concepts. Major requirements are all addressed and are well supported with quality evidence (eg. software screenshots, software files, hand calculations, references from literature). Content is comprehensive, accurate, and persuasive."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Demonstrates adequate understanding of the design procedure. Design may be inconsistent with regards to purpose and clarity of thought or feature errors. Major requirements are addressed but may lack some detail or are not well supported with evidence. Content may not be comprehensive and/or persuasive."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"Demonstrates minimal understanding of the design procedure. Major requirements are not addressed and/or lack evidence. Content is incomplete or not persuasive."}]},{"id":"C3","name":"Conclusion","weight":10,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"Detailed and appropriate conclusions reached from the evidence provided in the report."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Some conclusions are reached but they may be generic or lack specific detail."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"No actual or useful conclusions reached."}]},{"id":"C4","name":"Report presentation","weight":20,"max":null,"levels":[{"label":"Accomplished","min":7,"max":10,"desc":"The document follows a clear and logical engineering report structure. The report is easy to read: well-written, with good spelling and grammar, and appropriate language style. All aspects of formatting are consistent throughout the document. Executive summary (or abstract) provides a concise overview of the report. Graphical and tabular presentation of data is appropriate, clear and consistent. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are cited properly."},{"label":"Adequate","min":4,"max":6,"desc":"Document is not at a professional level but does make use of headings and subheadings to indicate document structure. The report may be difficult to read: writing is just ok, broad idea comes across; spelling and grammar have some flaws, not quite appropriate language style. Although figures and tables are labelled, the formatting is unclear and/or inconsistent to the extent that the reader can lose track of the context when reading. References in text match reference list (and vice versa) and are mostly cited correctly."},{"label":"Deficient","min":0,"max":3,"desc":"The document is poorly structured, is not coherent or shows a lack of understanding of the purpose of its sections. Much effort is required to read and understand the report: writing is poor, many mistakes with spelling and grammar, and possibly inappropriate language style (e.g. too informal). Presentation is poor to the extent that it impedes reading of the document. Examples include inconsistent formatting, and unlabelled figures or tables. References are either not cited or cited inconsistently."}]}]
MARKING:{"requirements":[{"id":"R1","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R2","met":"yes","where":"Mission Design"},{"id":"R3","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R4","met":"yes","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R5","met":"partial","where":"Introduction"},{"id":"R6","met":"yes","where":"Launch"},{"id":"R7","met":"yes","where":"Orbit"},{"id":"R8","met":"partial","where":"Orbit; Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R9","met":"yes","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R10","met":"yes","where":"Mission Duration & EOL"},{"id":"R11","met":"yes","where":"Orbit; Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R12","met":"partial","where":"Image 1; Image 2; Image 3; Image 4; Image 5"},{"id":"R13","met":"partial","where":"Executive Summary; Introduction; Mission Design; Conclusion; Bibliography"},{"id":"R14","met":"no","where":"Ground Control & Communications"},{"id":"R15","met":"partial","where":"Bibliography"}],"technical_issues":[{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report says RAAN indicates the location of the launch facility. RAAN specifies the inertial orientation of the ascending node; it is not determined by launch-site location alone, and the stated value is not justified by a launch-time or orbit-plane analysis."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"A sun-synchronous orbit does not inherently provide continuous sunlight to the spacecraft. Eclipse exposure depends on the orbit's local time of node and seasonal geometry; the report does not specify these or establish continuous illumination."},{"where":"Orbit","issue":"The report selects 600 km altitude and discusses a 250 km reef width but supplies no sensor field of view, swath, ground resolution, spectral capability, or target-access analysis to show that fluid-lensing observations are feasible or that useful reef coverage is achieved."},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","issue":"The access table repeats the 5 February 13:00 access as entries 14 and 15. This duplicate may inflate the stated access count and contributes to the reported statistics."},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","issue":"The drag-sail disposal concept has no sail sizing, decay-time estimate, or compliance demonstration. A passive drag sail is not an orbital manoeuvre, so the report's description of EOL as the mission's orbital manoeuvre is inconsistent."},{"where":"Launch","issue":"Vega E is described as unbuilt and its proposed schedule is speculative, yet it is selected without demonstrating a confirmed launch opportunity, rideshare availability, or delivery to the required orbit."}],"overall":"A coherent preliminary mission concept is presented with a plausible SSO and Australian ground facility, supported by STK screenshots and access results. Important design claims remain unverified, including reef imaging performance, launch compatibility, and EOL decay. Operator/customer roles, figure labelling, software files, and referencing also need improvement.","criteria":[{"id":"C1","level":"Adequate","mark":6.0,"scale":10.0,"points":6.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The report clearly explains the reef-monitoring mission, its environmental rationale, and why one spacecraft is proposed. It identifies potential government and scientific users, but does not clearly establish who operates the spacecraft or distinguish operators from customers. The rationale is useful but supported by limited, inconsistently presented references.","evidence":[{"where":"Introduction","quote":"Thus, only one satellite is required. This also just allows for simplicity in communications, reduces cost, and requires less time to build and launch."},{"where":"Introduction","quote":"The proposed satellite would likely be used by governmental and science agencies (e.g. CSIRO, BOM) for research and/or education."},{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"The satellite, nicknamed ZYZZsat (Z-axis/Y-axis Zeroed Zoning Satellite), is operated and accessed from Australia ground facilities"}]},{"id":"C2","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":30.0,"max":60.0,"reasoning":"The report presents a launch choice, orbit elements, STK screenshots/access results, a ground facility, and mission duration/EOL concepts. However, launch compatibility and timing are speculative, the RAAN rationale is incorrect, and target imaging capability and revisit are not demonstrated. The contact results contain a duplicated access, and EOL disposal is not quantitatively supported. Software files are not evident in the submission.","evidence":[{"where":"Launch","quote":"The proposed satellite will thus use the Vega E rocket as a launch vehicle – launched from the Guiana Space Centre Spaceport."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Specifically, an altitude of 600km requires a mean inclination of $ 97.7924^{\\circ} $ for the satellite to remain sun-synchronous."},{"where":"Orbit","quote":"Semi-major axis (a)</td><td>6971km"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"Systems Tool Kit 11 (STK) was used to calculate access periods as seen below:"},{"where":"Mission Duration & EOL","quote":"The most promising alternative is a ‘drag sail’ – a drag-enhancing deorbit device that deploys at the EOL."},{"where":"Image 3","quote":""}]},{"id":"C3","level":"Adequate","mark":5.0,"scale":10.0,"points":5.0,"max":10.0,"reasoning":"The conclusion recaps the selected launch, orbit, and ground facility and relates them to the mission purpose. It gives little synthesis of the design evidence or its limitations, and its claims about illumination and constant ground access are stronger than the analysis supports.","evidence":[{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"A polar sun-synchronous orbit allows for consistent illumination of the oceans while maintaining a constant sun angle."},{"where":"Conclusion","quote":"The Mt Pleasant Radio Observatory is well-located to receive data from this orbit type constantly and for long durations."}]},{"id":"C4","level":"Adequate","mark":4.0,"scale":10.0,"points":8.0,"max":20.0,"reasoning":"The report has a clear overall sequence, headings, an executive summary, and supporting images and data. However, writing and formatting are uneven, figures are not fully numbered and titled, and some in-text references do not match the bibliography. Wikipedia is used as a source despite the assignment's restriction.","evidence":[{"where":"Executive Summary","quote":"This report outlines the intended designs and procedures involved in the construction of a Earth observation satellite"},{"where":"Ground Control & Communications","quote":"DN path for orbits 1-15"},{"where":"Bibliography","quote":"[6] https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2021_in_spaceflight. [Online]."}]}]}
